WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.269 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in 2019  i served as deputy policy director for a  presidential  campaign working on a campaign is insane  and thrilling imagine a startup on  steroids  where reporters watch every move  you're racing towards a stressful  deadline  called election day each staffer is  responsible for a massive  amount of work and lots of work is done  by volunteers  i started as an unpaid advisor after  several months i became the first  full-time policy lead my job involved  building a team briefing the candidate  and developing plans for dozens  of issues one issue the candidate deeply  cared about  was racial equity one night the campaign  leadership called  they said hey we want a racial justice  plan to be our first  major release can you take it on  i had such mixed feelings about leading  this effort  i'd never directly experienced racism  i thought i don't know the solutions and  thinking that i do  would be so presumptuous  but this was also a chance to use my  power  to do something as a white person  it is my responsibility to fight for  racial justice  over generations my family and i have  benefited  from racism so it's absolutely  on us to help fix it  the question was how how could i design  an  equitable process could the campaign  have a real  impact so even if we lost we could push  other candidates to think bigger  i'm here to share five lessons i learned  from this process lesson one  make time i want to give you a sense of  how policies  often develop on campaigns campaigns can  assign an issue to whomever has time  and the staffer will write a few pages  of high-level points  or they'll copy and paste research from  a think tank  outsource to an expert or play it safe  and not release anything  but when it comes to racial justice we  needed to make time  when everyone is busy it's easy to avoid  the hardest work  so we started spending less time on  other projects to make room for this one  and plan for lots more calls with the  candidate  i had no idea how this process would go  but to do it right we needed to make  time  lesson two more voices  to create an equitable process i figured  we'd need a lot of voices involved  i'm grateful that two rock stars agreed  to help  nina smith was our traveling press  secretary and jose  morales our national constituency  director  they're both people of color with  backgrounds in policy  and government and they share many of  the lived and intersectional experiences  with the people we hoped to serve with  the plan  then we invited other extraordinary  experts  to join us porsha on civil rights  aaron on housing chike on  entrepreneurship  ashwin and andres on health and many  others  they are inspiring leaders of color  deeply  committed to these issues led by their  vision  together we set our sights high  we wanted to develop a plan as bold as  the marshall plan  that rebuilt europe after the second  world war  our goal a road map to dismantle  centuries  of racist systems across society  lesson three listen  then we had to get to work and actually  write a bold plan  the team started listening to so many  people's stories  within a month we talked to over 60  activists  scholars and organizations  formerly incarcerated individuals  fighting to restore  their right to vote academics  studying the racial wealth gap even the  direct descendants  of frederick douglass the more we  listened we heard creative ideas  and we saw our gaps for example we  talked to leaders in flint michigan as  they rebounded from the water crisis  it became clear we needed to be a lot  stronger  on environmental justice so we added  policies like  equitable disaster relief and making  sure the epa  considers environmental justice in all  of its decisions  listen broadly deeply  and with humility  lesson four stay engaged  this work can feel daunting systemic  racism runs  so deep across our institutions and  policies  the kinds of solutions we were seeking  were how to attack the racial wealth gap  reduce incarceration pass health policy  grounded  in anti-racism the more the scale of  these challenges sunk in the more i  realized that  i needed to stay engaged  it's easy to go vote protest  every once in a while and then turn away  staying engaged also meant educating  myself  sitting in meetings i realized wow there  is so much history  i'm missing so late at night i do deeper  dives on  redlining cash bail reparations  and many other issues our experts were  bringing to the table  to be an ally i needed to stay engaged  and constantly educate myself  lesson five be accountable  lots of organizations release diversity  and inclusion plans  they make commitments but what is the  plan  for accountability in our case it meant  making sure that racial equity was a  huge part of  every plan for example our disability  policy  was laser focused on equity  we addressed school suspension rates  voter suppression  unemployment all of these  disproportionately impact  people of color with disabilities  but as hard as we tried to center racial  equity  there were plenty of times i could have  done better  for example at the beginning of the  campaign i tracked diversity across  our volunteer teams i wanted to ensure  equitable recruitment and participation  but when i got busy i fell behind on  this effort  it's easy to stop following through  when we're crushed with work it's easy  to get discouraged when we get called  out  or make a mistake but there is no excuse  allyship isn't a mindset  it's actually doing the work  we ended up naming our platform the  frederick douglass plan  a comprehensive investment in the  empowerment  of black america and by we i mean the  dozens of leaders of color who drove  this vision  while there were lots of reactions to  the plan it shared bold ideas and  spotlighted racial justice as a campaign  priority i hold on to one reaction from  none other than  stacy abrams who said the douglas plan  should be  front and center in 2020  and beyond sure our candidate lost  but other candidates adopted our  recommendations  some were even carried out on day one of  the biden administration  so why am i sharing  this story of my learning process  because i want all of us to show up  for racial equity as you move into  senior roles your leadership especially  matters  our generation is facing so many complex  challenges  we need to think about racial equity as  we tackle problems  from climate change to income inequality  so let's make time  let's bring in more voices let's  listen and stay engaged  and let's hold ourselves accountable  otherwise the cracks in our democratic  project will keep growing  moving forward let's be as bold  and transformational as this  moment demands us to be  [Applause]  [Music]

